-- title:
--   Fibre estimate for the chaining ball, integrating in the first endpoint
-- statement:
--   Let $d \ge 1$, let $v \in \mathbb{R}^d$ be a unit vector, let $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$, and let $\alpha \ge 0$. As above,
--   $$m(s,t) \;=\; s + \frac{3\lVert s-t\rVert}{\sin\vartheta}\,v \qquad (\texttt{midCentre})$$
--   and the averaging set is $\bar B\big(m(s,t),\|s-t\|\big)$.
--
--   Fix now $t, z \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and integrate in the *first* endpoint:
--
--   $$\int_{\{s \,:\, z \in \bar B(m(s,t),\|s-t\|)\}} \|s-t\|^{-2d-\alpha}\,ds \;\le\; C'_{d,\vartheta,\alpha}\,\|z-t\|^{-d-\alpha}\cdot \omega_d ,$$
--
--   where
--   $$C'_{d,\vartheta,\alpha} \;=\; \frac{\bigl(2 + \tfrac{3}{\sin\vartheta}\bigr)^{2d+\alpha}}{\bigl(\tfrac{3}{\sin\vartheta} - 2\bigr)^{d}} \qquad (\texttt{chainConst\_prime})$$
--   is an explicit constant depending only on $d$, $\vartheta$ and $\alpha$, and $\omega_d = |\bar B(0,1)|$ is the volume of the unit ball of $\mathbb{R}^d$.
--
--   The centre $m(s,t)$ is anchored at $s$ and so is not symmetric in its two arguments: this is a genuinely separate statement from the version integrating in $t$, with its own constant $C'$ (the shifts $1$ and $-1$ of $C$ becoming $2$ and $-2$); both are needed, since the chaining argument produces one term anchored at each of the two endpoints.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** At $z = t$ the bound is **not** vacuous — it is the sharpest instance. The exponent $-d-\alpha$ is negative and `Real.rpow` sends $0$ to $0$ at a nonzero exponent, so the right-hand side is exactly $0$ there and the statement asserts that the fibre integral vanishes. It does: the fibre collapses to the single point $s = t$, since otherwise the triangle inequality forces the centre to be at distance at least $2\lVert s-t\rVert$ from $t$; and a point is null. The hypotheses $\|v\| = 1$, $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$, $\alpha \ge 0$ and $d \ge 1$ are all carried explicitly.
